# An IBM PC/AT-class machine — the board, not the firmware.
#
#   rsemu run pc-at --hd0 disk.img
#   rsemu run pc-at --bios /usr/share/qemu/bios.bin
#   rsemu run pc-at --bios bios.bin --media vgabios=vgabios.bin --media floppy=boot.img
#
# **rsemu ships no *third-party* BIOS and never will.** A PC's firmware is
# somebody's copyrighted binary, and vendoring one would be redistribution; so
# the machine takes it through a media slot, exactly as `riscv-virt` takes its
# firmware, and the user points at their own copy. Running a binary as an
# emulated guest is ordinary use whatever its licence; shipping it is not
# (ROADMAP.md §1).
#
# What the `bios` slot now has is a **default**, not a replacement:
# `src/fw/pcbios` is rsemu's own minimal legacy BIOS, assembled from Rust source
# in this repository by `src/fw/asm16`, so the first line above boots with
# nothing supplied. `--bios` still wins. The reason it exists at all is that
# FreeDOS, Windows 95 and Windows XP need a *legacy* BIOS, the one permissively
# licensed PC firmware in the world is UEFI, and every legacy alternative is GPL
# and therefore unreadable to us — ROADMAP.md phase 6a names it as a
# deliverable rather than an option.
#
# Nothing below assumes *which* firmware. The board is the AT's and the
# addresses are the AT's, and any firmware written for that board will find what
# it expects — or will say what is missing, which is the more useful outcome
# while this machine is being brought up.
#
# # Sources
#
# The IBM Personal Computer AT Technical Reference (1984) for the board: which
# chip answers which port, which IRQ each device lands on, and the memory map.
# The Intel data sheets for the 8259A, 8254, 8237A and 8042; the Motorola
# MC146818 and MC6845 data sheets; the NEC uPD765A data sheet; Ralf Brown's
# Interrupt List for the ports the data sheets leave to the board.
#
# For the parts a 1984 AT did not have: the Intel 82441FX datasheet (order
# 290549-001) for the host bridge and its PAM registers; Intel SDM Volume 3A
# chapter 10 for the local APIC's register page at 0xfee00000; the Intel 82093AA
# datasheet for the I/O APIC's at 0xfec00000; the IA-PC HPET Specification
# rev 1.0a for the HPET's at 0xfed00000; and the MultiProcessor Specification
# §3.6.2.1 for the IMCR at 0x22/0x23 and §5.1 for which I/O APIC input each
# legacy line reaches. No emulator source was consulted for any of it.

machine "pc-at" {
  # -- what the user can change -----------------------------------------------

  # Base memory: the 640 KiB below the video hole. Not a parameter anybody
  # should want to change, but the RTC has to be told the same number and this
  # is how it gets told once.
  param basemem = 640K

  # Memory above 1 MiB. Zero is a legitimate machine — an AT with 1 MiB fitted.
  param extmem = 15M

  # The system BIOS window. 128 KiB at 0xe0000 is what an AT-class board with a
  # modern firmware decodes; a 64 KiB image sits in the top half of it and a
  # 128 KiB image fills it, because `pc.rom` aligns a short image against the
  # top where the reset vector is.
  param biosbase = 0xe0000
  param biossize = 128K

  # The video option ROM window. 64 KiB at 0xc0000, which is where firmware
  # starts its scan for a `0x55 0xaa` signature.
  param vgabase  = 0xc0000
  param vgasize  = 64K

  # Which host character port the keyboard's scan codes arrive on, so two of
  # these can run in one process without typing at each other.
  param keyboard = "keyboard"

  # The two drive bays on the primary IDE channel. Zero is an empty bay, and an
  # empty bay is the default, because a PC with no hard disk is an ordinary PC
  # and because a drive costs its whole capacity in host memory the moment it
  # exists. A non-zero size fits a blank drive; binding an image to the `hd0`
  # or `hd1` media slot fills one; and a size of zero with an image bound takes
  # the image's own length, which is the usual way in:
  #
  #   rsemu run pc-at --bios bios.bin --media hd0=disk.img
  #   rsemu run pc-at --bios bios.bin --param hd0size=64M
  #
  # The secondary channel is wired below and both of its bays are empty. A
  # machine file that wants a drive there copies `hd0` and says
  # `bay = "ide1-master"`.
  param hd0size = 0
  param hd1size = 0

  # The date and time the RTC starts at. A device may never read the host
  # clock (CLAUDE.md, determinism): rsemu has no record/replay seam for it, and
  # a machine that drew from one would not be reproducible. So it is declared,
  # and two runs of this file see the same Tuesday.
  param time = "2026-01-01T00:00:00"

  # -- crystals ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Six, because the board has six, and they are six separate trees rather
  # than dividers off one because they are five separate cans (ROADMAP.md §4.2).
  # Only the ratios *within* a tree are guest-visible; the absolute frequencies
  # set the wall-clock rate and nothing architectural.

  # The CPU. 25 MHz is a 386DX of the period. Nothing depends on the number.
  osc cpu = 25000000 Hz

  # The 8254's input, and the most consequential number on the board: it is
  # 14.31818 MHz / 12 = 105/88 MHz exactly, and it is *not an integer number of
  # hertz*, which is precisely why the language takes rational literals. Every
  # DOS timing loop ever written is calibrated against it.
  osc pit = 105000000/88 Hz

  # The RTC's watch crystal. 32.768 kHz is 2^15, which is why the periodic
  # interrupt rates are all powers of two.
  osc rtc = 32768 Hz

  # The VGA's dot clock. A real card has **two** cans — 25.175 MHz for the
  # 640x480 modes and 28.322 MHz for the 720x400 text mode — and the
  # miscellaneous output register's clock-select bits choose between them at
  # runtime. A clock domain has one parent, so the board wires the device to the
  # text-mode crystal, which is what it comes out of reset selecting and the
  # only one that matters until a graphics mode exists. Divided by 9 for the
  # nine-pixel character cell; the device counts character clocks.
  osc dot = 28322000 Hz

  # The 8042's own crystal. Divided down to a byte rate: an AT keyboard's serial
  # line runs at 10-16.7 kHz and a frame is eleven bits, so a scan code takes
  # about a millisecond, and one tick of this domain is one opportunity to move
  # one byte between the keyboard and the host. The scheduler is the only thing
  # allowed to decide when that happens (`CLAUDE.md`: a device never reads the
  # wall clock).
  osc kbd = 12000000 Hz

  # The floppy controller's data separator. Nothing here divides by it; the
  # controller completes its operations without modelling their duration, and
  # says so in its own documentation.
  osc fdd = 1000000 Hz

  # The processor's front-side bus, which is what a local APIC's timer counts:
  # SDM Vol 3A §10.5.4 says the timer counts "the processor's bus clock or core
  # crystal clock frequency ... divided by the value in the divide configuration
  # register". 100 MHz is a period-appropriate number and nothing architectural
  # depends on it.
  osc bus = 100000000 Hz

  # The HPET's own crystal. 10 MHz, so one tick is 100 ns — and the device is
  # told the same number a second time, in femtoseconds, because it reports the
  # period to the guest and cannot see its own domain's rate. The two must
  # agree: 10^15 / 10^7 = 10^8 fs.
  osc hpet = 10000000 Hz

  # -- address spaces ---------------------------------------------------------

  # 32 bits, because this board is meant for a 386. A real-mode-only core
  # simply never drives an address above 0x10ffff, and this costs it nothing.
  #
  # `unassigned = read-as-ones`: an unmapped read returns ones. That is what an
  # ISA bus with nothing driving it does, and firmware **depends** on it —
  # sizing memory, scanning for option ROMs and probing for adapters all work by
  # reading a hole and recognising 0xff. `fault` would be a more honest default
  # for a machine we were debugging, and the wrong model of this one.
  #
  # Not `open-bus`, which now means something narrower and more literal: the
  # value left on the *master's own* data bus by its last cycle. That is right
  # for a 6502, whose bus floats and decays; on an AT the ISA bus has pull-ups
  # and genuinely reads as ones, which is a different claim about different
  # silicon.
  space mem { width = 32, unassigned = read-as-ones }

  # The separate I/O space the 8086 family drives with its own status lines. 16
  # bits, though an AT decodes only ten and every port below therefore aliases
  # every 0x400 — an alias nothing uses, so it is not modelled and this is the
  # comment saying so.
  space port { width = 16, unassigned = read-as-ones }

  # -- the processor ----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # An **80486**, named as such, because that is the part this board is wired
  # for and a machine file describes a board rather than a preference. The
  # evidence is above and below this line: a 32-bit `mem` space, a 25 MHz clock
  # whose own comment calls it "a 386DX of the period", the ROM decoded a
  # second time just under 4 GiB so that a reset vector at 0xfffffff0 finds it,
  # an ELCR at 0x4d0/1, fast A20 at 0x92, reset control at 0xcf9, and an IDE
  # cable. Not one of those is on a 1984 PC/AT. `pc-at` names the *lineage* —
  # the AT's port map, its two cascaded 8259As, its 8042, its CMOS layout —
  # and not the part in the socket, exactly as every "PC/AT-compatible" 486
  # clone did.
  #
  # A 486 and not a 386 because current firmware executes `CPUID`, which a 386
  # answers with #UD. If a 286-era board is ever wanted it should be a second
  # machine file with its own memory map and no high ROM alias, not a property
  # on this one.
  #
  # The class is `cpu.x86` rather than `cpu.i8086`: they are one core and the
  # names differ only in what an unwritten `model` would mean (an 8088 under
  # the older name, an 80486 under this one). `model` is written out anyway, so
  # neither default is load-bearing.
  #
  # `iospace` names the second space, because `space =` is structural and there
  # is only one of it. A core with no I/O space reads ones and discards writes,
  # which is what an unpopulated bus does — and would make every chip below
  # invisible, so it is named explicitly rather than defaulted.
  object cpu0 "cpu.x86" {
    clock   = cpu
    space   = mem
    iospace = "port"
    model   = "80486"
    engine  = "interp"
  }

  # -- memory -----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Declared before anything that writes into memory, because `Machine::reset`
  # runs devices in declaration order and a cold reset zeroes RAM.
  #
  # Two objects, not one, because there is a hole between them: 0xa0000-0xfffff
  # is video memory, option ROMs and the system BIOS. A single 16 MiB region
  # would sit under all of them and the priority rules would decide the machine
  # by accident.
  object ram_low  "ram" { size = basemem }
  object ram_high "ram" { size = extmem }

  # -- the host bridge, and the RAM that shadows the ROM ----------------------
  #
  # An Intel 82441FX (PMC), the north bridge of the 440FX PCIset. That is a 1996
  # Pentium Pro part on a board called `pc-at`, and the reason is the same one
  # that made this board's processor a 486 (see the CPU above): `pc-at` names
  # the AT's *lineage* — its port map, its two cascaded 8259As, its 8042, its
  # CMOS layout — and not a 1984 parts list. A firmware written this century
  # will not get past its own initialisation on a board with no host bridge, and
  # it says so out loud: "Unable to unlock ram - bridge not found", and then it
  # sizes RAM, stores the answer in a variable in its own f-segment, and reads
  # back zero because that segment is a ROM socket.
  #
  # Unlocking that segment is the PMC's **PAM** registers, at configuration
  # offsets 0x59-0x5f: thirteen windows across 0xc0000-0xfffff, each with a read
  # enable and a write enable, choosing per window and per direction between the
  # ROM on the expansion bus and 256 KiB of main memory (Intel 82441FX
  # datasheet, order 290549-001, §3.2.18). `src/dev/pc/pmc.rs` quotes the
  # section.
  #
  # That 256 KiB belongs to *this* object rather than being a third `ram` object
  # because the PMC is the DRAM controller as well as the bridge — that is what
  # the two halves of its name mean — and because a machine file cannot hand one
  # device a reference to another's store (`docs/platforms/pc-at.md`, "framework
  # gaps"). `space = mem` is how it is told which decode it governs.
  #
  # A 440FX is a PCI chipset, so this also brings the 0xcf8/0xcfc configuration
  # window with it. The bus it is the root of is empty: no slots, no south
  # bridge, no video. Firmware finds a host bridge, finds nothing else, and says
  # so — which is the correct answer for this board and a better one than the
  # master abort at 0xcf8 it used to get.
  #
  # `passthrough = sysctl` is the awkward part of this board, and it is a real
  # chipset fact rather than a modelling wrinkle. `CONFADD` is four bytes at
  # 0xcf8 that the *north* bridge claims only for a Dword access, while the byte
  # at 0xcf9 — the reset control register — belongs to the *south* bridge and is
  # claimed only by a byte access. Two chips, one address, told apart by the
  # byte enables. An rsemu address space decodes by address alone, so the chip
  # that needs all four bytes holds them and hands the narrow cycles on, which
  # is 82441FX §3.1.1's own word for it: a byte or word reference "passes
  # through the Configuration Address Register to the PCI Bus" — and the south
  # bridge is on that bus. `pc.sysctl` publishes those four bytes as it decodes
  # them, this names it as the source, and 0xcf9 is therefore *not* mapped
  # separately below.
  object pmc "pc.pmc" { space = mem, bus = "pci0", passthrough = sysctl }

  # -- interrupts -------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Two 8259As. The slave's INT output drives the master's IR2, which is what
  # makes IRQ8-15 exist at all, and the acknowledge cycle follows the same net
  # backwards: `core::wire`'s `IntAck` is how the master asks the slave for the
  # vector during the second INTA pulse, and how the CPU asks the master.
  #
  # The vector bases are the AT's: 0x08 for IRQ0-7 and 0x70 for IRQ8-15. They
  # are not written here — firmware programs them through ICW2, and a machine
  # file that pre-set them would be describing software.
  object pic1 "pc.pic" { mode = "master" }
  object pic2 "pc.pic" { mode = "slave" }

  # -- interrupts, the APIC half ----------------------------------------------
  #
  # A local APIC in front of the processor, an I/O APIC for the board's lines to
  # land on, and the **IMCR** that decides which of the two paths the 8259A's
  # output takes. All three are *additions*: nothing above changes, and a
  # firmware that has never heard of an APIC boots on this board exactly as it
  # did before, because the MP specification says a board powers up in PIC mode
  # and this one does.
  #
  # That is the whole reason `pc.imcr` exists rather than the machine file
  # simply moving `pic1.int` to `lapic0.lint0`. Out of reset a local APIC is
  # software-disabled and every local vector table entry reads as masked (SDM
  # Vol 3A §10.4.7.2), so a board wired straight to LINT0 has no timer tick
  # until firmware switches the APIC on and programs LINT0 for `ExtINT`.
  # SeaBIOS does that; rsemu's own BIOS does not, and neither did DOS. The
  # hardware answer to exactly this problem is the MP specification's
  # §3.6.2.1 interrupt mode configuration register, and it is a chip on the
  # board rather than a bit in any of these three.
  #
  # `id = 0` and the implied `bsp = true`: this is the bootstrap processor. The
  # I/O APIC takes the next id, which is what the MP specification's I/O APIC
  # entry publishes and what an operating system addresses it by.
  object lapic0 "pc.lapic"  { clock = bus, id = 0, bus = "apic" }
  object ioapic "pc.ioapic" { id = 1, bus = "apic" }
  object imcr   "pc.imcr"   {}

  # -- the timer --------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Counter 0 is the system tick on IRQ0, counter 1 drove DRAM refresh, and
  # counter 2 is gated by port 0x61 and drives the speaker — and its output is
  # readable back in bit 5 of that same port, which is how firmware calibrates
  # a delay loop without a reference clock.
  object pit0 "pc.pit" { clock = pit }

  # The HPET's three timers, on I/O APIC inputs 20, 21 and 22 — inputs with no
  # ISA line behind them, which is what makes them available. Each `routeN`
  # says which input its pin reaches, and that is what the timer's
  # `Tn_INT_ROUTE_CAP` advertises to the guest: the property and the `wire`
  # below are two halves of one fact and must agree.
  #
  # `LEG_RT_CNF`, the legacy replacement route that disconnects the 8254 from
  # IRQ0 and the RTC from IRQ8, is a gate between three chips rather than a
  # register in any of them, so it is not modelled here — see the wires.
  object hpet0 "pc.hpet" {
    clock  = hpet
    period = 100000000
    route0 = 20
    route1 = 21
    route2 = 22
  }

  # -- the real-time clock and CMOS -------------------------------------------
  #
  # The memory sizes are written here as well as being the `ram` objects' sizes,
  # because a device cannot read the machine's memory map — `BindCtx` carries a
  # space but not a picture of what is in it. That is a seam to close, not a
  # design: the RISC-V board's device tree generator solves the same problem by
  # reading the realized machine, and a PC's CMOS is the same question asked in
  # 1984.
  object cmos "pc.rtc" {
    clock   = rtc
    time    = time
    basemem = basemem
    extmem  = extmem
  }

  # -- the keyboard controller ------------------------------------------------
  #
  # An 8042, which is a microcontroller with its own firmware; what is modelled
  # is the interface, not the program. It owns the A20 gate and the system reset
  # line, which is why leaving protected mode goes through a keyboard chip.
  object kbc "pc.kbc" { clock = kbd / 12000, port = keyboard }

  # -- the system control ports -----------------------------------------------
  #
  # Not a chip: latches and gates on the board. Port B at 0x61 gates the timer's
  # counter 2 and reads its output back; port A at 0x92 is the chipset's fast
  # path to A20 and reset, which is why both signals have two drivers.
  object sysctl "pc.sysctl" {}

  # -- DMA --------------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Two 8237As. The first moves bytes on channels 0-3; the second moves words on
  # channels 4-7 and its channel 4 is the cascade the first hangs off. Both are
  # bus masters, so both declare the space their transfers traverse
  # (ROADMAP.md §4.4).
  object dma1 "pc.dma" { space = mem, mode = "byte", base = 0 }
  object dma2 "pc.dma" { space = mem, mode = "word", base = 4 }

  # -- video ------------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # A CRTC, a character buffer and a character generator. Text mode only, which
  # is what reaches a prompt; the picture comes out through the `host::display`
  # scanout seam, so `--screenshot` and the browser see what the guest drew.
  # `dot-clock` says the same number the domain above does, and has to: the
  # device derives its frame period from its own registers and needs the
  # crystal's rate to do it, and `BindCtx` carries a clock domain but not the
  # forest's picture of what that domain is rated at. Written twice, like the
  # RTC's memory sizes and for the same reason — a seam to close, not a design.
  object vga "pc.video" { clock = dot / 9, dot-clock = 28322000 }

  # The PCI face of that same adapter, and the reason a firmware written this
  # century puts anything on the screen at all.
  #
  # A 440FX-era firmware does not find video by scanning memory. It enumerates
  # the bus looking for class code 030000 — display, VGA-compatible — and takes
  # that function's video BIOS off its **expansion ROM base address register**
  # (PCI Local Bus Specification Rev 2.1 §6.2.5.2). It cannot do anything else
  # here: the first thing it does to the chipset above is set PAM1-PAM5 to
  # read/write, which turns 0xc0000-0xdffff into blank DRAM and makes the legacy
  # `vgarom` socket below invisible from that moment on. That window is the
  # firmware's scratch pad for the ROMs it copies *into* it, and this is where
  # one of them comes from.
  #
  # Two objects for one card, and the seam is the same one `pc.ide` and
  # `ata.disk` sit either side of: `pc.video` is the CRTC, the character buffer
  # and the character generator, reached through the board's own decode of
  # 0x3c0-0x3df and 0xb8000 exactly as an ISA card is; this is what answers a
  # configuration cycle. A PCI VGA's legacy aperture is not addressed through
  # its BARs either, so nothing is lost by splitting them.
  #
  # `vendor-id` and `device-id` are not decoration. A video BIOS built for a PCI
  # card carries a PCI Data Structure naming the ids it is for, and a firmware
  # that loads a ROM off a BAR checks them; a BIOS built for an ISA card carries
  # none and cannot be loaded this way at all. The defaults match the common
  # PCI-VGA image, and a different image wants different numbers here.
  object vgacard "pc.vga-pci" {
    space  = mem
    bus    = "pci0"
    device = 2
    image  = "vgabios"
  }

  # -- the floppy controller --------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The `floppy` media slot is optional: a machine with no disk in the drive is
  # an ordinary machine, and the controller reports "not ready" rather than
  # faulting.
  object fdc "pc.fdc" { clock = fdd, image = "floppy" }

  # -- the hard disks and the IDE channels ------------------------------------
  #
  # The drives are declared *before* the channels, and the order matters:
  # `Machine::reset` runs devices in declaration order, and an IDE channel's
  # only job at reset is to re-drive its interrupt pin from whatever the drives
  # now say. A channel reset before its drives would hold a stale level until
  # the first port access.
  #
  # "IDE" means the controller is on the drive, which is why there are two
  # kinds of object here rather than one: `ata.disk` is everything on the far
  # side of the cable — the command set, the registers, the geometry — and
  # `pc.ide` is what the motherboard contributes, which is address decode, the
  # master/slave cable position and one wire to the second 8259A. The same
  # drive would hang off a different adapter unchanged, which is the test of
  # whether the line is in the right place.
  object hd0 "ata.disk" { image = "hd0", size = hd0size, bay = "ide0-master" }
  object hd1 "ata.disk" { image = "hd1", size = hd1size, bay = "ide0-slave" }

  object ide0 "pc.ide" { master = "ide0-master", slave = "ide0-slave" }
  object ide1 "pc.ide" { master = "ide1-master", slave = "ide1-slave" }

  # -- firmware ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # Both sockets take a user-supplied image and neither ships with one. `align`
  # is the difference that matters: the system BIOS sits against the *top* of
  # its socket because that is where the reset vector is, and the video option
  # ROM against the *bottom* because that is where the scan for `0x55 0xaa`
  # looks.
  #
  # `bios` is the one slot with a default, and the default is ours:
  # `rsemu::fw::pcbios::image()` is 64 KiB, so it lands in the top half of this
  # 128 KiB socket with its reset vector at 0xffff0 and at 0xfffffff0 through
  # the alias below. Anything the user binds displaces it.
  #
  # `vgarom` is the **legacy** path to the same image the PCI card above
  # carries, and it is kept because it is the only path a pre-PCI firmware
  # knows: a scan of 0xc0000 upward for the signature. A firmware that
  # programs the PAM registers first never sees it, and takes the PCI route
  # instead. One video BIOS, two ways in, and any given firmware takes exactly
  # one of them — rsemu's own takes the legacy one, because it does not
  # reprogram the PAM registers and does not enumerate the bus.
  object bios   "pc.rom" { image = "bios",    size = biossize, align = "top" }
  object vgarom "pc.rom" { image = "vgabios", size = vgasize,  align = "bottom" }

  # -- the memory map ---------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # 0x00000  base RAM, up to the video hole
  # 0xa0000  video memory
  # 0xc0000  the video option ROM
  # 0xe0000  the system BIOS
  # 0x100000 extended memory
  # 0xfffe0000 the system BIOS again — see below
  map mem 0x00000000 size basemem  = ram_low
  map mem 0x000b8000 size 0x8000   = vga.vram      # the colour text page
  map mem vgabase    size vgasize  = vgarom
  map mem biosbase   size biossize = bios
  map mem 0x00100000 size extmem   = ram_high

  # The three APIC-era register pages, at the addresses their specifications
  # give as the reset default. None of them is relocatable here: moving the
  # local APIC's page is `IA32_APIC_BASE`, and this build's x86 core has no
  # `WRMSR` to write it with.
  map mem 0xfec00000 size 0x0020   = ioapic.regs   # 82093AA §3.1
  map mem 0xfed00000 size 0x0400   = hpet0.regs    # HPET spec §3.2.4
  map mem 0xfee00000 size 0x1000   = lapic0.regs   # SDM Vol 3A §10.4.4

  # The same ROM chip, decoded a second time at the top of the 32-bit address
  # space. A 386 fetches its first instruction from 0xfffffff0 with CS's hidden
  # base still pointing there, and only reaches 0x000f0000 once firmware has
  # made its first far jump. Both windows are one chip, which is why the region
  # is named twice rather than duplicated.
  map mem 0x100000000 - biossize size biossize = bios

  # -- the I/O map ------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The AT's, and written once. Each device below knows only offsets within its
  # own block.
  map port 0x0000 size 0x0010 = dma1.regs      # DMA 1, channels 0-3
  map port 0x0020 size 0x0002 = pic1.regs      # master 8259A
  map port 0x0022 size 0x0002 = imcr.regs      # the MP spec's IMCR, §3.6.2.1
  map port 0x0040 size 0x0004 = pit0.regs      # 8254
  map port 0x0060 size 0x0001 = kbc.data       # 8042 data
  map port 0x0061 size 0x0001 = sysctl.portb   # speaker, timer 2 out, refresh
  map port 0x0064 size 0x0001 = kbc.cmd        # 8042 status and command
  map port 0x0070 size 0x0002 = cmos.regs      # RTC index (with the NMI mask)
  # The page latches. On a real AT these are one file of 74LS612-style latches
  # serving both controllers, and the offsets are not in channel order — 0x87 is
  # channel 0, 0x81 is channel 2, 0x8b is channel 5. rsemu gives each controller
  # its own latch file and hands it the half that holds its own channels'
  # bytes, which is why the second one is aliased at offset 8: no page byte is
  # ever read by a controller that does not own that channel, so splitting the
  # file changes nothing observable and saves inventing a third device for
  # sixteen bytes of latch.
  map port 0x0080 size 0x0008 = alias(dma1.pages, 0)   # channels 0-3, and 0x80
  map port 0x0088 size 0x0008 = alias(dma2.pages, 8)   # channels 5-7
  map port 0x0092 size 0x0001 = sysctl.porta   # fast A20 and fast reset
  map port 0x00a0 size 0x0002 = pic2.regs      # slave 8259A
  map port 0x00c0 size 0x0020 = dma2.regs      # DMA 2, channels 4-7, at 2x
  map port 0x03b4 size 0x0002 = vga.crtc-mono  # the CRTC, monochrome address
  map port 0x03c0 size 0x0010 = vga.vga        # sequencer, DAC, attribute...
  map port 0x03d4 size 0x0002 = vga.crtc-colour # ...and its colour address,
                                              # and which of the two answers is
                                              # the miscellaneous output
                                              # register's bit 0, not the board
  map port 0x03d8 size 0x0002 = vga.mode       # CGA mode and colour select
  map port 0x03da size 0x0001 = vga.status     # retrace, and the AC flip-flop
  # The IDE channels. Each decodes in two disjoint places, which is why each
  # publishes two regions rather than one: eight ports for the command block,
  # and one port for the control block, whose alternate status register is how
  # a driver looks at a drive without acknowledging its interrupt.
  map port 0x0170 size 0x0008 = ide1.regs      # IDE, secondary channel
  map port 0x01f0 size 0x0008 = ide0.regs      # IDE, primary channel
  map port 0x0376 size 0x0001 = ide1.ctl       # ...and its control block
  # The floppy adapter's window has a hole in it at 0x3f6, and the hole is the
  # board's doing rather than ours: the diskette adapter decodes 0x3f0-0x3f5
  # and 0x3f7, and 0x3f6 belongs to the fixed-disk adapter. 0x3f7 is genuinely
  # shared on a real AT — the floppy drives bit 7 and the disk adapter bits 6:0
  # — and only the floppy's half is modelled, because the other half is a
  # drive-address register nothing reads.
  map port 0x03f0 size 0x0006 = fdc.regs       # uPD765A, 0x3f0-0x3f5
  map port 0x03f6 size 0x0001 = ide0.ctl       # IDE, primary control block
  map port 0x03f7 size 0x0001 = alias(fdc.regs, 7)  # DIR read, CCR write
  map port 0x04d0 size 0x0001 = pic1.elcr      # edge/level control, master
  map port 0x04d1 size 0x0001 = pic2.elcr      # edge/level control, slave

  # PCI configuration, mechanism #1: the address latch at 0xcf8 and the data
  # window at 0xcfc, one eight-byte decode because that is one decode on the
  # board (82441FX §3.1.1, §3.1.2).
  #
  # This window swallows 0xcf9, the chipset's reset control register and the
  # third way to reboot a PC — firmware writes 0x02 then 0x06 there and the
  # reset happens on bit 2's rising edge. It still works: the bridge hands that
  # byte to `sysctl` through the `passthrough` link above, which is what a real
  # north bridge does with a cycle it does not claim. Mapping `sysctl.resetctl`
  # here as well would put two devices at one address, and the flattener would
  # split every Dword write to 0xcf8 into three pieces around it.
  map port 0x0cf8 size 0x0008 = pmc.config

  # -- the wires --------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # The interrupt tree. The slave cascades onto the master's IR2, and the
  # master's INT reaches the processor through the IMCR — which out of reset
  # passes it straight to `cpu0.intr`, so this is the AT's wiring with a switch
  # in it rather than a different board. The acknowledge travels back down the
  # same nets, and the IMCR forwards it: `INTR` carries a level *and* a vector.
  wire pic2.int    -> pic1.ir2
  wire pic1.int    -> imcr.int
  wire imcr.intr   -> cpu0.intr
  wire imcr.lint0  -> lapic0.lint0

  # The local APIC's own output, wire-ORed onto the same pin. Two drivers on
  # `cpu0.intr`, and **the order of these two `wire` statements is load
  # bearing**: both offer an acknowledge handler, the processor asks them in
  # declaration order, and a local APIC never declines a cycle — SDM Vol 3A
  # §10.9 makes the spurious vector the defined answer to "you asked and there
  # is nothing". So the IMCR is asked first, and it declines whenever the
  # register says the APIC owns the pin. Swap the two lines and a PIC-mode
  # machine takes every interrupt with the spurious vector.
  wire lapic0.intr -> cpu0.intr
  wire lapic0.nmi  -> cpu0.nmi

  # Every legacy line lands twice: on the 8259A input it has always had, and on
  # the I/O APIC input the MP specification assigns it. That is how the boards
  # were actually built — the same pin goes to both parts — and it is what lets
  # an operating system switch from one controller to the other at run time by
  # masking the 8259A and unmasking the redirection entry.
  #
  # IRQ0 is the exception the MP specification calls out: the 8254's output
  # reaches I/O APIC input **2**, not input 0, because input 0 is where an
  # ExtINT entry for the whole 8259A cascade would go (MP spec §5.1).
  wire pit0.out0   -> pic1.ir0    # the system tick
  wire pit0.out0   -> ioapic.irq2
  wire kbc.irq1    -> pic1.ir1    # a key was pressed
  wire kbc.irq1    -> ioapic.irq1
  wire fdc.irq     -> pic1.ir6    # the floppy controller
  wire fdc.irq     -> ioapic.irq6
  wire ide0.irq    -> pic2.ir6    # IRQ14, the primary IDE channel
  wire ide0.irq    -> ioapic.irq14
  wire ide1.irq    -> pic2.ir7    # IRQ15, the secondary IDE channel
  wire ide1.irq    -> ioapic.irq15
  wire cmos.irq    -> pic2.ir0    # IRQ8, the RTC
  wire cmos.irq    -> ioapic.irq8
  wire kbc.irq12   -> pic2.ir4    # IRQ12, the auxiliary device
  wire kbc.irq12   -> ioapic.irq12

  # The HPET's three comparators, on the inputs its `routeN` properties
  # advertise. They land on the I/O APIC and nowhere else: an HPET is not an ISA
  # part and has no 8259A input of its own.
  #
  # What is *not* here is the legacy replacement route. Setting `LEG_RT_CNF` on
  # a real board disconnects the 8254 from IRQ0 and the RTC from IRQ8 and puts
  # HPET timers 0 and 1 there instead — a gate between three chips, which is
  # neither a register in the HPET nor one in the 8259A. The gates exist now
  # (`wire.and` and `wire.not` are ordinary objects, ROADMAP.md §4.3), but the
  # HPET has no output pin saying whether the bit is set, so there is nothing to
  # wire their enable to. That pin is the remaining piece; until it exists the
  # bit reads back and does nothing, which is what `pc.hpet` documents.
  wire hpet0.t0    -> ioapic.irq20
  wire hpet0.t1    -> ioapic.irq21
  wire hpet0.t2    -> ioapic.irq22

  # The timer's counter 2 and the board's port B: software gates the counter
  # through bit 0 and reads its output back in bit 5. Counter 1's output is the
  # refresh toggle in bit 4.
  wire sysctl.gate2 -> pit0.gate2
  wire pit0.out2    -> sysctl.timer2
  wire pit0.out1    -> sysctl.refresh

  # A20 and reset each have two drivers — the keyboard controller and the
  # chipset's fast port — which is exactly the wired-OR a net with two sources
  # exists for (ROADMAP.md §4.3).
  wire kbc.a20      -> cpu0.a20
  wire sysctl.a20   -> cpu0.a20
  wire kbc.reset    -> cpu0.reset
  wire sysctl.reset -> cpu0.reset

  # The floppy controller's DMA request lands on channel 2. The bytes travel
  # the same net in the other direction, through `core::wire`'s `DmaPeripheral`.
  wire fdc.drq      -> dma1.dreq2
}
